nts is associated with a lower risk of developing DLBCL.\n   - **Above 200/mm\u00b3**: In this stratum, no significant relationship was found between cART adherence and DLBCL risk (Relative Risk = 0.96, p = 0.", "Infectious Diseases": "The medical scenario presented involves a follow-up analysis of a study focusing on the relationship between antiretroviral therapy (cART) adherence and the risk of developing diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL) in HIV-positive patients, stratified by CD4+ T-lymphocyte count at the time of diagnosis. The key findings are:\n\n1. Among patients with CD4+ cell counts below 200/mm\u00b3, cART adherence was significantly associated with a reduced risk of DLBCL (Relative Risk [RR] = 0.52, p = 0.01). 2. Among patients with CD4+ cell counts above 200/mm\u00b3, no significant relationship was found between cART adherence and DLBCL risk (RR = 0.96, p = 0.36). ### Interpretation of the Condition\n\nThe condition being depicted is the relationship between cART adherence and the risk of DLBCL in HIV-positive patients, with a focus on how this relationship varies based on the patient's CD4+ T-lymphocyte count at the time of HIV diagnosis. CD4+ T-lymphocyte count is a critical marker of immune function in HIV infection, and lower counts are associated with more advanced disease and higher risk of opportunistic infections and cancers. ### Noteworthy Aspects\n\n1..", "Oncology": "The medical scenario presented involves a follow-up analysis of a study focusing on the relationship between cART (combination antiretroviral therapy) adherence and the risk of developing DLBCL (Diffuse Large B-Cell Lymphoma) in HIV-positive patients, stratified by CD4+ T-lymphocyte count at the time of diagnosis.\n\n### Interpretation of the Condition:\n1. **HIV and CD4+ T-lymphocyte Count**: The CD4+ T-lymphocyte count is a critical marker of immune function in HIV-infected individuals. A count below 200 cells/mm\u00b3 is considered a marker of advanced HIV disease and is